 ## Overview
-Morton Code Encoding/Decoding converts N-dimensional integer coordinates into a single 1D integer index (the Morton code) by interleaving the bits of each coordinate component, and reverses this process to recover the original coordinates [S1, S3, S7, unofficial]. This linearization preserves spatial locality, mapping nearby points in N-dimensional space to nearby indices in a 1D array, which accelerates cache-coherent access in volume rendering and sparse voxel octree construction [S5, unofficial][S6][S7, unofficial]. It sets itself apart from hierarchical indexing methods (like [Morton Code Generation for Octrees](4673-morton-code-generation-for-octrees.md)) by operating on flat Cartesian coordinates rather than tree paths, and from lookup-table variants by providing the fundamental bit-interleaving definition [S3, S7, unofficial].
