 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Time Complexity:** Naive encoding/decoding is $O(\log V)$ where $V$ is the max coordinate value (number of bits). Fast bit-interleaving (parallel bits) is $O(1)$ with a constant number of bitwise operations (typically 5-10 shifts/masks per dimension) [S2, S8, unofficial]. Lookup table methods are $O(1)$ but require memory allocation for tables [S3, unofficial].
-*   **Memory Complexity:** $O(1)$ for naive/fast methods. Lookup table methods require $O(D \cdot 2^B)$ memory where $D$ is dimensions and $B$ is bits per byte/segment [S3, unofficial].
+*   **Time Complexity:** Naive encoding/decoding is $O(B)$ where $B$ is the number of bits per coordinate. Fast bit-interleaving (parallel bits) is $O(1)$ with a constant number of bitwise operations (typically 5-10 shifts/masks per dimension) [S2, S8, unofficial]. Lookup table methods are $O(1)$ but require memory allocation for tables [S3, unofficial].
+*   **Memory Complexity:** $O(1)$ for naive/fast methods. Lookup table methods require $O(D \cdot \text{tableSize})$ memory where $D$ is dimensions and `tableSize` is the maximum coordinate value + 1 [S3, unofficial].
 *   **Performance:** Fast bit-interleaving is significantly faster than naive looping [S2, S3, unofficial]. Lookup tables can be faster on CPUs with high cache hit rates for small tables, but may suffer from cache misses for large tables [S3, unofficial].
 *   **Strengths:** Preserves spatial locality, enabling efficient spatial hashing, grid traversal, and neighbor lookups [S5, unofficial][S6][S7, unofficial]. Simple to implement [S7, unofficial].
